      passage: `The Keep's best teachers believed that students should struggle with material before receiving explanations. [Consequently], they withheld answers and allowed students to sit with confusion for days before intervening. This approach appeared cruel to outside observers. [Moreover], the students who experienced it reported that their eventual understanding was deeper and more durable than anything they could have achieved by being given the answers immediately. The teachers called this method "the productive friction."`,
